## STEP 7 — TEST DATA LAYER

### Why we have `types.ts`
- JSON files only tell TypeScript what the data IS right now
- Interface defines what the data MUST ALWAYS BE (a contract)
- If JSON changes and breaks the shape, TypeScript catches it at compile time
- Enables reuse across factory functions, API responses, page objects

### `testdata/types.ts`
```typescript
export interface Credentials {
  username: string;
  password: string;
}

export interface RegistrationData {
  name:     string;
  email:    string;
  password: string;
  state:    string;
  hobbies:  string[];
}

export interface CourseData {
  courseName:     string;
  description:    string;
  instructorName: string;
  price:          string;
  category:       string;
  thumbnail:      string;
}
```

### `testdata/credentials.ts` — reads from environment, not JSON
```typescript
export const credentials = {
  username: process.env['ADMIN_EMAIL']    ?? '',
  password: process.env['ADMIN_PASSWORD'] ?? '',
}

if (!credentials.username || !credentials.password) {
  throw new Error('\n[Config Error] ADMIN_EMAIL or ADMIN_PASSWORD not set in config/.env\n')
}
```

### Why credentials moved out of JSON into `.env`
- JSON files committed to git are visible to everyone with repo access
- Git history is permanent — deleting the file does not remove it from history
- Bots scan GitHub for committed credentials and exploit within minutes
- Rule: if you would need to rotate it (change it), it does not belong in git

