## STEP 10 — FIXTURES (Dependency Injection)

### What fixtures do
- Instead of writing `new LoginPage(page)` in every test — fixtures inject it
- `test.extend()` creates a custom test object with all page objects pre-wired
- Code BEFORE `await use()` = setup (like beforeEach)
- Code AFTER `await use()` = teardown (guaranteed to run even if test throws)
- ALL tests import `test` from fixture.ts — never from `@playwright/test` directly

### Key architectural decision — why categoryPage is NOT a fixture
- CategoryPage wraps a POPUP TAB, not the main page
- The popup tab does not exist until after login + navigation
- Fixtures are resolved BEFORE the test body runs — before login
- So categoryPage must be created manually in the test body
- Use `try/finally` in the test for guaranteed popup tab cleanup

### `fixtures/fixture.ts`
```typescript
import { test as base, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import { LoginPage }        from '../pages/LoginPage';
import { RegistrationPage } from '../pages/RegistrationPage';
import { DashboardPage }    from '../pages/DashboardPage';
import { CoursePage }       from '../pages/CoursePage';

type CustomFixtures = {
  loginPage:        LoginPage;
  registrationPage: RegistrationPage;
  dashboardPage:    DashboardPage;
  coursePage:       CoursePage;
};

const test = base.extend<CustomFixtures>({

  loginPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    await use(new LoginPage(page));
    // DO NOT call page.close() here
    // page is shared across all fixtures in the same test
    // closing it here breaks every other fixture
  },

  registrationPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    await use(new RegistrationPage(page));
  },

  dashboardPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    await use(new DashboardPage(page));
  },

  coursePage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    await use(new CoursePage(page));
  },

});

export { test, expect };
```

