## ARCHITECTURAL DECISIONS EXPLAINED

### Why TypeScript over JavaScript
- Catches bugs at compile time not runtime
- `strict: true` prevents null/undefined errors
- Interfaces enforce data shapes — JSON drift caught immediately
- Better IDE autocomplete and refactoring support

### Why Fixtures over beforeEach
- `beforeEach` with `new LoginPage(page)` repeated in every test = duplication
- Fixtures inject page objects automatically — tests just declare what they need
- Teardown in fixture is GUARANTEED — `afterEach` can be skipped if test panics
- Type-safe — TypeScript knows exactly what each fixture provides

### Why BasePage
- DRY principle — `goto`, `assertVisible`, `waitForVisible` written once
- All pages inherit a full toolkit
- Change behavior in one place — affects all pages
- Beginners skip this and duplicate Playwright calls across every page

### Why separate `types.ts`
- JSON auto-inference is not a contract
- Interface is the contract — protects against shape changes
- One definition reused across tests, factories, API responses
- Documents required vs optional fields explicitly

### Why `cross-env` for npm scripts
- `ENV=STAG command` is Linux/Mac only
- On Windows it silently fails — tests run against wrong environment
- `cross-env ENV=STAG command` works on all operating systems

### Why `npm ci` not `npm install` on CI
- `npm install` can update versions, causing non-reproducible builds
- `npm ci` installs exactly what is in `package-lock.json` — guaranteed reproducibility
- Faster on CI because it skips dependency resolution

